                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">140</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160-170</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbs / fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6 g / 4 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 g / 2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9-10 g / not listed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g / 1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g (0 g added)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Acesulfame K, sucralose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk protein isolate, whey concentrate, calcium caseinate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk protein concentrate, calcium caseinate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey concentrate, whey isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate, whey concentrate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Contains</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk, soy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk, soy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milk</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price per shake or serving (Amazon)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.58</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.23</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.27</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.03</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.15</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.86</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.27</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.69</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Pick Pure Protein for the fewest calories. Pick Premier for price, flavors and the better powder.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pick Pure Protein if you want the most protein per calorie, a bit more fiber, or a shake whose allergen line lists milk only. Pick Premier Protein if you want the lower price per shake, the widest flavor range, a caffeinated coffee option or a powder with 30 g of protein and 3 g of carbs per serving. Pick neither if you are a drug-tested athlete; neither brand carries a sport certification we could find.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            These are close rivals. Both shakes are milk-based, both are sweetened with the same pair of artificial sweeteners and neither is lactose-free, so the differences come down to 20 calories, a couple of grams of fiber, the price on the day and which flavors you like. The powders are a different story: Premier's tub beats Pure Protein's on protein per serving, carbohydrate and cost per gram. If you want the wider field of bottled shakes, our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink guide</a> ranks them all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Pure Protein and Premier Protein Products Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            One card per like-for-like product: each brand's 30 g chocolate shake in a 12-pack and each brand's main whey powder in chocolate. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on 18 November 2026 and change often, so treat the cost per 25 g as a snapshot. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary slightly by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Are Pure Protein and Premier Protein the Same Company?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No. Pure Protein and Premier Protein are made by two unrelated companies. Premier Protein belongs to BellRing Brands, the company that also owns Dymatize. Pure Protein's shake cartons say they are manufactured for 1440 Foods, the company that also sells MET-Rx, Body Fortress and FitCrunch, per its own website. Pure Protein is not owned by Simply Good Foods, which owns Quest and Atkins, a mix-up that comes up often.
          </p>

          <p>
            The confusion is understandable. The two names are similar, both brands sit side by side in the same supermarket fridge and pharmacy aisle, both sell 30 g shakes in 11 oz bottles and both sell a whey powder. Pure Protein started as a protein bar brand and was part of The Bountiful Company's sports nutrition group before that group was sold off, while Premier built its name on cases of shakes at warehouse clubs. Our <a href="/pure-protein-powder-review/">Pure Protein powder review</a> covers the brand history in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The ownership matters for one practical reason: the two ranges are built differently. Premier is a shake company first. It sells dozens of shake flavors, a protein coffee line, clear protein drinks, a fiber powder and breakfast products, and its powder is designed to taste like the shake. Pure Protein is a bar company first. Its shake range is smaller, around eight flavors on its own site at the time of writing, and its powder line is limited to chocolate and vanilla.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither company publishes a third-party sport certification for these products, and neither shares the exact split of its protein blends. Everything below comes from the nutrition panels and ingredient lists on the brands' own pages and retailer listings, read at the time of writing. Labels change, so check the panel on the pack you buy, especially if you have an allergy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Has More Protein, Pure Protein or Premier Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pure Protein and Premier Protein shakes have the same protein: 30 g per bottle, per both labels at the time of writing. That is 60 percent of the daily value on both panels and enough for a full post-workout or meal-replacement dose for most adults. Neither brand sells a higher-protein bottle in its core range, so on grams alone the shakes tie.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein sources differ slightly. Pure Protein's Complete shake uses a blend of milk protein isolate, whey protein concentrate and calcium caseinate. Premier uses milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate, with no separate whey. Milk protein is roughly 80 percent casein and 20 percent whey, so both shakes are casein-heavy, which is part of why they feel thick and filling. Pure Protein's added whey concentrate nudges its blend a little closer to a classic post-workout profile, but the difference is small.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both blends are complete proteins with all nine essential amino acids, and both supply plenty of leucine at 30 g of dairy protein, comfortably above the roughly 2.5 g per serving that research links with a strong muscle-building response. Neither label breaks out leucine or BCAAs, so treat any exact figure you see elsewhere as an estimate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powder is where protein differs. Premier's 100% Whey gives 30 g per two-scoop serving, while Pure Protein's 100% Whey gives 25 g per single scoop. That is not a fair head-to-head on its own, because the serving sizes differ. Per gram of powder, Premier is about 73 percent protein and Pure Protein about 60 percent, which is the number that tells you how much filler comes with the protein. The powder section below covers it in full.
          </p>

          <p>
            If 30 g still is not enough, look elsewhere rather than doubling up on sweetened cartons. Our <a href="/muscle-milk-vs-premier-protein/">Muscle Milk vs Premier Protein</a> comparison covers the 33 g Muscle Milk Pro carton, and the best protein drink guide lists the 42 g Fairlife Core Power bottle.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories-sugar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has Fewer Calories, Less Sugar and Fewer Carbs?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pure Protein has fewer calories: 140 per bottle against Premier's 160, per the labels at the time of writing. With the same 30 g of protein in each, that puts about 86 percent of Pure Protein's calories in protein, against about 75 percent for Premier. Per 25 g of protein, Pure Protein costs you about 117 calories and Premier about 133.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of the gap is fat. Pure Protein lists 2 g of fat and 0.5 g of saturated fat; Premier lists 3 g of fat and 1 g of saturated fat. Twenty calories a day is small, but if you drink a shake every day on a cut it adds up to about 600 calories a month, roughly a meal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sugar is effectively a tie. Pure Protein lists under 1 g of total sugar, including under 1 g of added sugar, plus under 1 g of sugar alcohol. Premier lists 1 g of total sugar and 0 g of added sugar. Pure Protein's ingredient list does include a small amount of sugar and maltodextrin, which Premier's does not, but the totals per bottle are below the level that matters for blood sugar for most people.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carbohydrate splits the other way. Pure Protein lists 6 g of carbohydrate, of which 4 g is fiber, which the carton itself works out as 2 g of net carbs. Premier's chocolate carton lists 4 g of carbohydrate with 2 g of fiber, also about 2 g net. So on net carbs they are level, and Pure Protein simply carries more fiber. Both suit a low-carb or keto plan, and Pure Protein's Amazon listings describe the shake as keto-friendly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Per bottle, per the label</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">140</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pure Protein calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">160</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Premier calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Pure Protein fiber</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Premier fiber</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sodium is close too: 240 mg for Pure Protein chocolate and 230 mg for Premier chocolate, about a tenth of the daily value. Pure Protein lists 650 mg of calcium, half the daily value, which is useful for anyone who drinks the shake instead of milk. Premier advertises 24 vitamins and minerals on the front of the carton; Pure Protein's panel lists a similarly long run of added vitamins and minerals, including vitamins A, C, D, E and zinc, without making it the headline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Do Pure Protein and Premier Use Artificial Sweeteners?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, both do, and they use the same two: sucralose and acesulfame potassium. That is true of both shakes and both powders, per the ingredient lists at the time of writing. If you want to avoid artificial sweeteners, switching from one brand to the other changes nothing. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners guide</a> lists stevia, monk fruit and unsweetened options instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond the sweeteners, the shake labels are long and similar. Pure Protein's chocolate shake lists water, its protein blend, cocoa, maltodextrin, natural and artificial flavors, cellulose gel, sugar, salt, cellulose gum, sunflower oil, sunflower lecithin and a vitamin and mineral premix. Premier's lists filtered water, milk protein concentrate, calcium caseinate, cocoa, under 1 percent sunflower or soybean oil, natural and artificial flavors, cellulose gel and gum, salt, carrageenan, phosphate salts and its own premix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Allergens and carrageenan</h3>

          <p>
            The allergen lines differ. Pure Protein's shake carton says it contains milk ingredients and is made in a facility that also processes tree nuts, soy and fish. Premier's says it contains milk and soy, because its oil can be soybean oil. If you avoid soy strictly, Pure Protein's shake is the safer read, although the shared-facility warning still applies. The powders flip this: Premier's powder is labelled soy-free and Pure Protein's powder contains soy lecithin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier lists carrageenan, a seaweed-derived thickener; Pure Protein's shake uses cellulose gel and gum instead. Carrageenan is approved for food use, but some people prefer to avoid it and a few report stomach upset with it. If that is you, it is a small point in Pure Protein's favor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither shake is a clean-label product, and neither pretends to be. They are shelf-stable, artificially sweetened and fortified. That is fine for most healthy adults. Anyone with a milk allergy should avoid both, and anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant or who takes medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a 30 g shake to every day. This page is label analysis, not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

