        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pure Protein and Premier Protein shakes are closer than their marketing suggests. Both give 30 g of protein in an 11 oz bottle, both use sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and both are casein-heavy milk shakes with near-zero sugar. Pure Protein is the leaner bottle, at 140 calories with 4 g of fiber and a milk-only allergen line. Premier is the cheaper and more varied one, at about $1.86 per 25 g at the Amazon price we saw, with far more flavors.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Pick Pure Protein if calories and fiber come first, and Premier if price and flavor choice do. If you drink shakes at home, skip both bottles and buy Premier's powder, which gave 30 g of protein for about $1.69 per 25 g, well below Pure Protein's tub. For the full field of bottled shakes, see our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drink rankings</a>.
